"Sharp Bound of the Third-Order Hankel Determinant for a Symmetric Domain (https://www.mdpi.com/2073-8994/18/8/1307)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Sharp Bound of the Third-Order Hankel Determinant for a Symmetric Domain ... This paper presents a new approach to the third-order Hankel determinant problem for the family of starlike functions associated with a symmetric H-domain.\n\n# Sharp Bound of the Third-Order Hankel Determinant for a Symmetric Domain\n\nby\n\nAdel Salim Tayyah\n\nImageAdel Salim Tayyah\n\nSciProfiles Scilit Preprints.org Google Scholar\n\n^{ 1,*}^{},\n\nSarem H. Hadi\n\nImageSarem H. Hadi\n\nSciProfiles Scilit Preprints.org Google Scholar\n\n^{ 2}^{} and\n\nAlina Alb Lupaş\n\nImageAlina Alb Lupaş\n\nSciProfiles Scilit Preprints.org Google Scholar\n\n^{ 3,*}^{}\n\n^{1}\n\nDepartment of Cybersecurity, College of Computer Science and Information Technology, University of Al-Qadisiyah, Diwaniyah 58002, Iraq\n\n^{2}\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, College of Education for Pure Sciences, University of Basrah, Basrah 61001, Iraq\n\n^{3}\n\nDepartment of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Oradea, 410087 Oradea, Romania\n\n^{*}\n\nAuthors to whom correspondence should be addressed.\n\nSymmetry 2026, 18(8), 1307; https://doi.org/10.3390/sym18081307\n\nSubmission received: 8 July 2026 / Revised: 29 July 2026 / Accepted: 31 July 2026 / Published: 3 August 2026\n\n(This article belongs to the Section B: Mathematics)\n\nDownload keyboard_arrow_down\n\nDownload PDF\nDownload PDF with Cover\nDownload XML\nDownload Epub\n\nBrowse Figure\n\nVersions Notes\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis paper presents a new approach to the third-order Hankel determinant problem for the family of starlike functions associated with a symmetric H-domain. Motivated by a recently proposed sharp bound, we develop an analytical method that substantially improves the best known estimate. The obtained upper bound is reduced from 0.104485 to 0.062581, which is remarkably close to the conjectured sharp constant 1/16=0.0625. This significant improvement provides strong evidence supporting the validity of the conjectured bound while introducing a systematic technique for deriving refined coefficient estimates. Furthermore, the proposed approach is sufficiently flexible to be extended to related extremal problems involving Hankel determinants and other subclasses of analytic functions in geometric function theory.\n\nKeywords:\n\nanalytic functions; starlike functions; convex functions; coefficient bounds; Hankel determinants\n\nMSC:\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe Hankel determinant {\\mathbb{H}}_{q,n}\\left(f\\right), with positive integers n and q, based on the analytic function\n\nf\\left(\\xi \\right)=\\xi +{\\displaystyle \\sum _{n=2}^{\\infty }}{b}_{n}\\phantom{\\rule{0.166667em}{0ex}}{\\xi }^{n},\\phantom{\\rule{1.em}{0ex}}\\xi \\in \\mathbb{U},\n\n(1)\n\nis formulated as\n\n{\\mathbb{H}}_{q,n}\\left(f\\right)=\\left|\\begin{array}{cccc}{b}_{n}& {b}_{n+1}& \\cdots & {b}_{n+q-1}\\\\ {b}_{n+1}& {b}_{n+2}& \\cdots & {b}_{n+q}\\\\ ⋮& ⋮& \\ddots & ⋮\\\\ {b}_{n+q-1}& {b}_{n+q}& \\cdots & {b}_{n+2q-2}\\end{array}\\right|.\n\nThe behavior of {\\mathbb{H}}_{q,n}\\left(f\\right) for different pairs (q,n) has been extensively investigated within several subfamilies of univalent functions. In particular, Janteng et al. [1] inferred sharp bounds for the second Hankel determinants in the starlike and convex families, whereas Krishna et al. [2] derived the optimal estimates of {\\mathbb{H}}_{2,2}\\left(f\\right) in the Bazilevi\\stackrel{̆}{\\mathrm{c}} class. Hadi et al. [3] continued to investigate the constraints of Hankel determinants relevant to the exponential function.\n\nThe third-order determinant is expressed as\n\n{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)=2{b}_{2}{b}_{3}{b}_{4}-{b}_{3}^{3}-{b}_{4}^{2}+{b}_{3}{b}_{5}-{b}_{2}^{2}{b}_{5}.\n\n(2)\n\nZaprawa [4] investigated the third Hankel determinant and obtained non-sharp bounds, namely |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 1 for starlike functions and |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 49/540 for convex functions. Since the formula of {\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right) involves higher-order coefficients, deriving exact estimates is technically demanding, and achieving sharpness becomes essential. Despite these difficulties, Kowalczyk et al. [5] succeeded in 2018 in establishing the sharp bound |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 4/135 for the convex class. For starlike functions, the same upper estimate |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 4/135 was later reported by Kwon et al. [6]. In the same year, Lecko et al. [7] demonstrated that the sharp bound |{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le 1/9 holds for starlike functions of order 1/2. The following Table 1 provides a survey of results concerning the computation of the third-order Hankel determinant for various classes of functions. It indicates whether the proposed bound is sharp, and if not, whether a conjecture for the sharp bound was formulated and subsequently proved by another researcher. We highlight the cases in which the bound was not sharp but a conjecture for the sharp bound was proposed, yet remained unproven. In this paper, we establish the proof of one such conjecture, while leaving our methodology available to researchers for addressing the other conjecture, which likewise remains an open problem.\n\nLupaş et al. [17] introduced the following class of functions, which is also presented in Table 1:\n\n{S}_{\\mathrm{Sym}}^{\\ast }:=\\left\\{f\\in \\mathcal{A}:{\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\xi {f}^{\\prime }\\left(\\xi \\right)}{f\\left(\\xi \\right)-f(-\\xi )}}={\\phi }_{H}\\left(\\omega \\left(\\xi \\right)\\right),\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{for}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{some}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\omega \\in \\mathbb{B},\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{and}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{every}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\xi \\in \\mathbb{U}\\right\\},\n\nwhere this class is characterized via the analytic function {\\phi }_{H}:\\mathbb{U}\\to \\mathbb{C} (see Figure 1), which satisfies the following formula\n\n{\\phi }_{H}\\left(\\xi \\right)=1+\\xi +{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}}{\\xi }^{2}-{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}}{\\xi }^{3},\\phantom{\\rule{1.em}{0ex}}\\xi \\in \\mathbb{U}.\n\n(3)\n\nSee also [20].\n\nThey presented several sharp inequalities, such as those for the initial coefficients, the Fekete–Szegö inequality, and the second-order Hankel determinant. However, they provided the best possible upper bound for the third-order Hankel determinant, namely 0.104485, which is not sharp. At the same time, they proposed the following conjecture, which is sharp:\n\nConjecture 1 \n\n([17], p. 12). If f\\in {\\mathcal{S}}_{\\mathrm{Sym}}^{\\ast }, then the sharpness of the third Hankel bound is given by\n\n|{\\mathbb{H}}_{3,1}\\left(f\\right)|\\le {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{16}}.\n\nThe earlier approach in Lupaş et al. [17] provided a significant estimate by applying coefficient inequalities and a parametrization of Carathéodory functions. In the present work, we refine this strategy in three respects:\n\nAn interior critical point must satisfy the complete system\n\n{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial \\mathsf{\\ell }}}=0,\\phantom{\\rule{2.em}{0ex}}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial u}}=0,\\phantom{\\rule{2.em}{0ex}}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial v}}=0.\n\nSolving the third equation yields v={v}_{p}. Hence, we substitute {v}_{p} into the remaining two equations and obtain\n\n{\\left.{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial \\mathsf{\\ell }}}\\right|}_{v={v}_{p}}=0,\\phantom{\\rule{2.em}{0ex}}{\\left.{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial u}}\\right|}_{v={v}_{p}}=0.\n\nThe conditions 0<{v}_{p}<1 imply inequalities (16) and (17). However, a direct analysis of the two reduced equations shows that they cannot 