order Hankel determinant was also established, accompanied by a conjecture for its sharp bound to stimulate further research. This work opens several avenues for future investigations, including the study of higher-order Hankel determinants, proving the stated conjecture, or exploring analogous function classes within the same domain. We believe these results provide valuable contributions and serve as a foundational reference for subsequent studies related to this domain.\n\n## Author Contributions\n\nConceptualization and initial idea, A.A.L.; research design and methodology development, A.A.L., A.S.T. and J.S.; software implementation and technical development, A.S.T.; data curation and validation, A.A.L. and J.S.; formal analysis and interpretation, A.S.T.; investigation and experimentation, A.A.L., A.S.T. and J.S.; resources acquisition, J.S.; data organization and management, A.S.T.; original draft preparation, A.S.T.; manuscript review and editing, A.A.L. and J.S.; visual representation and illustrations, A.S.T.; supervision and academic guidance, J.S.; project administration, J.S.; funding acquisition, A.A.L. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA study of sharp coefficient bounds for a new subfamily of starlike functions | Journal of Inequalities and Applications | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13660-021-02729-1)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In particular, the following determinants are known as the first-, the second-, and the third-order Hankel determinants, respectively: ... In the literature, there are just a few references to the Hankel determinant for functions belonging to the general family \\(\\mathcal{S}\\). ... The challenge of finding the sharp bounds of Hankel determinants for a particular family of functions drew the attention of numerous researchers.\n----------------------------------------------------------